          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Burning when I pass urine, plus a temperature and back pain since yesterday. How urgent is this, and what does guidance describe for treatment?",
          "answer": "**Please get a medical assessment today, not at a routine appointment.** Burning when you pass urine, a fever, and back pain together can mean a urinary infection that has spread to the kidney. Contact a doctor, urgent care, or a nurse advice line now. Do not wait to see whether it passes. The automated check labelled your situation \"routine\" only because none of its fixed emergency patterns matched, which is not a clearance.\n\n**Call emergency services immediately** if you have any of these: confusion, unusual drowsiness, or slurred speech; fast or hard breathing; a fast heartbeat or dizziness when standing; not passing urine for many hours; skin that is mottled, very pale, or blue, or a rash that does not fade when pressed; shivering, feeling very cold, or a very high temperature; or vomiting that stops you keeping fluids down, or severe back or side pain. Also fainting, or feeling rapidly worse. If you could be pregnant, tell the clinician today, because the guidance I could retrieve does not cover pregnancy.\n\nA urinary tract infection is the condition the retrieved guidance matches most closely, so it is what a clinician would check first. This is not a diagnosis, and only a clinician can examine you and test a urine sample.\n\nOn treatment, the sources differ in what they could find. One search returned no treatment guidance for your combination of symptoms. Another retrieved a summary for simple bladder infections in non-pregnant adults. It lists nitrofurantoin, trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole and fosfomycin as commonly described first-line options, but it says a suspected kidney infection follows a different pathway with different antibiotic choices and durations. So those options do not answer your situation, and I cannot say which antibiotic is right for you. A clinician will decide on testing and treatment, including whether you need hospital care. Bring a list of your symptoms and when they started.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
